      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p11", "s1:p14", "s1:p15", "s1:p16"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 180413,
    "summary": "A flowering shrub of northern North America and Greenland, bog Labrador tea grows mainly in cold, acidic wetlands. Its evergreen leaves have leathery, wrinkled tops and densely hairy undersides, while its small white flowers form fragrant clusters. The leaves are brewed as tea, though concentrated doses may be toxic to humans.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "Leaves are traditionally brewed as fragrant tea; concentrated doses may be toxic to humans.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p2", "s1:p7", "s1:p9"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p1", "s1:p7", "s1:p9"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 320319,
    "summary": "A large, upright columnar cactus from South America, the Venezuelan apple cactus has grey-green to blue stems with variable spines that may be absent. Its large cream flowers open for one night, and its thornless fruits contain sweetening white flesh and crunchy edible seeds.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "The thornless fruit has edible white flesh and small crunchy seeds; the flesh sweetens when the fruit opens fully.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p3", "s1:p4"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p2", "s1:p4"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 68225,
    "summary": "A deciduous tree native from China through Southeast Asia to the Indian subcontinent, the orchid tree bears conspicuous pink or white flowers. Its mature pods twist into corkscrews and burst open with an audible clack. In South Asia, its edible buds are cooked in curries, stir-fried or pickled, while flowers and buds make Nepalese pickles.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "Buds are cooked in curries, stir-fried or pickled across South Asia; flowers and buds are used in Nepalese pickles.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p2", "s1:p4", "s1:p5", "s1:p11", "s1:p13"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p10", "s1:p11", "s1:p13"]
    }
  },
